  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will build across the region over the next couple\nof days providing just about the best weather you can hope for\nto end the month of July. The only fly in the ointment will be\npatchy valley fog each night. This could be locally dense this\nmorning and again Friday morning, especially along the rivers \nand larger creeks with a fairly large delta between the cool air\ntemperatures and warm water temperatures. The fog should be less\nof an issue Friday night with increasing high clouds and a \nwarmer night expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA positively titled shortwave swings into and across the Ohio \nValley Saturday night into Sunday, perhaps even closing off \nover or near our region early next week. This brings an increase\nin shower and thunderstorm activity late Saturday through the\nday on Sunday as PWATs surge to around 2 inches. WPC has been \nhighlighting the western portions of our CWA in their Excessive \nRainfall Outlooks and could really see this be at least a \nlocalized issue just about anywhere Saturday night into Sunday. \nThe severe thunderstorm threat appears limited, however, as the\nstrongest kinematics push through Saturday night, largely out \nof phase with the peak diurnal instability on Sunday.\n\nLonger range models then differ on the speed with which the \nupper system exits our region and the NBM maintains shower/ \nthunderstorm chances all the way through midweek, especially \neach afternoon and evening.",
